\section{Abstract Viewing Screen}
\label{sec:abstract-screen}

When the user presses Enter on a paper, we show a full-screen view of the
abstract along with all paper metadata. This screen allows the user to read
the full abstract and make a decision about the paper.

\subsection{Screen Lifecycle and Navigation}

Textual screens form a stack. When we [[push_screen]], the new screen
appears on top; when we [[dismiss]], we return to the previous screen.
This matches the natural mental model: drilling into a paper (push),
then returning to the list (dismiss).

The abstract screen provides several actions:
\begin{description}
\item[Keep (\texttt{K})] Quick keep---mark paper as kept, return to list; a
  paper kept already keeps its themes, any other gets none
\item[Themes (\texttt{T})] Keep with themes---open theme selection modal, then return
\item[Discard (\texttt{d})] Open motivation selection modal, then return
\item[Notes (\texttt{n})] Open external editor for paper notes
\item[PDF (\texttt{p})] Download and open the paper's PDF
\item[Enrich (\texttt{e})] Fetch missing abstract from Semantic Scholar or OpenAlex
\item[Back (Escape/q)] Return to list without changing status
\end{description}

The distinction between \texttt{K} (quick keep) and \texttt{T} (keep with themes)
lets a reviewer keep a paper with one key and add themes later, or
choose the themes at once with \texttt{T}.

\subsection{LLM Decision Handling}
\label{sec:abstract-llm-handling}

When reviewing papers that have been pre-classified by an LLM (marked with
[[LLM_UNREVIEWED]] source), any keep or discard action in this screen will
update the review source appropriately. If the user confirms the LLM's decision,
the paper is marked as [[LLM_REVIEWED]]. If the user changes the decision (for
example, keeping a paper that the LLM had discarded), the paper is also marked
as [[LLM_REVIEWED]] and flagged with [[is_example=True]], which puts it
first among the examples of later LLM rounds when it is tagged.

An AI agent's unreviewed decisions ([[AGENT_UNREVIEWED]], recorded with
[[sessions decide --source agent]]) are handled the same way and become
[[AGENT_REVIEWED]]: every check here asks
[[ReviewSource.is_unreviewed]] rather than naming the LLM, and
[[mark_as_reviewed]] picks the reviewed source that keeps the machine's
name (\cref{sec:review-source}).

The main review screen handles these decisions the same way, so a
machine decision is marked reviewed (and, when changed, flagged) whether
the user decides it from the paper list or from the detailed abstract
view.

Every keep, discard and confirmation in either screen is a human's
decision, so each also keeps the record of the human's review
([[record_human_decision]] with the channel [[tui]],
\cref{sec:human-review-record} in the review module): the time, and the
reviewer's name only when the reviewer gave one, with [[--by]] of
[[sessions resume]] or on entering blind mode
(\cref{sec:tui-blind-review}); a login guessed here would put a name on
a record its owner never made.  The record is written
after [[mark_as_reviewed]], because it describes the decision that
stands, whose source must by then be a human's or a reviewed one.  A
decision recorded through the Python API, by contrast, gets no record,
which is what lets a report tell the two apart.

\paragraph{Closure capture for asynchronous state.}
The key insight is that we must capture the LLM state \emph{before} any modal
dialogs open, since the user's selection in the modal determines the final
decision. When working with modal dialogs in TUI frameworks like Textual, the
callback pattern introduces a timing challenge: variables like [[was_llm]] and
[[was_discarded]] must be captured \emph{before} the modal opens, since the
dialog is asynchronous and the decision object might change between the modal
opening and the callback executing.

Python closures solve this elegantly. When we define a nested function like
[[handle_themes]] inside [[action_keep_with_themes]], the function captures
references to local variables from the enclosing scope. By reading [[was_llm]]
and [[was_discarded]] before calling [[push_screen]], we freeze these values at
the moment the action was initiated---exactly when we need to know whether the
user is changing an LLM decision.

\subsection{External Editor Integration}

For note editing, we use [[self.app.suspend()]] which temporarily releases
the terminal back to the shell, allowing the user's preferred editor (from
[[VISUAL]] or [[EDITOR]] environment variables) to run. This leverages
familiar tools rather than implementing a text editor in the TUI.

\subsection{Normalising the Abstract Text}

Most providers store the abstract as a plain string.  However, some
providers---most notably Web of Science---parse the abstract body from XML
and may return a \emph{list} of paragraph strings when the abstract contains
more than one paragraph.  Although this is a data-layer inconsistency (the
[[Paper.abstract]] field is typed [[str | None]]), it can occur in practice
whenever a cached or externally sourced paper carries a list value.

Rather than crash at render time with a [[TypeError]], we normalise
the value here in the view layer as a last line of defence.  Paragraphs are
joined with a blank line ([[\n\n]]) so that the rendered text preserves the
original paragraph breaks.

<<tui abstract helpers>>=
def normalize_abstract(abstract) -> str:
    """Return abstract as a plain string, joining list paragraphs if needed.

    Some providers return the abstract as a list of paragraph strings.
    This helper joins them with blank lines so paragraph breaks are
    preserved in the TUI display.
    """
    if isinstance(abstract, list):
        return "\n\n".join(str(p) for p in abstract if p)
    return abstract or ""
@

We can verify both cases without needing a running Textual application:

<<test functions>>=
class TestNormalizeAbstract:
    """Tests for the normalize_abstract helper."""

    def test_string_abstract_is_returned_unchanged(self):
        """normalize_abstract() passes through a plain string."""
        assert normalize_abstract("Just one paragraph.") == "Just one paragraph."

    def test_list_abstract_is_joined_with_blank_lines(self):
        """normalize_abstract() joins paragraphs with blank lines."""
        result = normalize_abstract(["First.", "Second."])
        assert result == "First.\n\nSecond."

    def test_none_abstract_returns_empty_string(self):
        """normalize_abstract() treats None as an empty string."""
        assert normalize_abstract(None) == ""

    def test_empty_paragraphs_are_skipped(self):
        """normalize_abstract() ignores empty strings in the list."""
        result = normalize_abstract(["First.", "", "Third."])
        assert result == "First.\n\nThird."
@

