\section{Filter Modal}
\label{FilterModal}

The filter narrows the list to a subset of papers: for example only the
kept papers, to write notes on them, or only the pending ones.

The filter modal provides four filter options:
\begin{description}
\item[All] Shows all papers regardless of decision status (the default view).
\item[Kept] Shows only papers marked for keeping, useful for focused note-taking 
  or generating a report of selected papers.
\item[Discarded] Shows only discarded papers, helpful for reconsidering previous 
  rejections.
\item[Pending] Shows only papers awaiting decision.
\end{description}

We use the same keybindings as the main decision actions for consistency: 
\texttt{K} for kept (matching the keep action) and \texttt{d} for discarded 
(matching the discard action), so a key means the same status in the
list and in the filter.

A further filter, [[s]], lists the papers of a review sample
(\cref{sec:llm-sample} in the LLM review module) that still wait for
review, in the order they were drawn.  [[scholar llm sample]] draws the
sample and records it in the session; this filter is how the reviewer
works through it, and each paper leaves the list as soon as it is
reviewed, which is also when it starts counting in the sample's report.

The filter [[x]] lists the papers on which two screeners disagree and
no human has yet settled it (\cref{sec:agreement-conflicts} in the
agreement module): status and category conflicts among the screenings
recorded beside the decisions.  Settling one is deciding the paper, so
the reviewer keeps, discards or confirms it as usual, and the paper
leaves the list, since the human's decision is then newer than the
screenings.  A paper a human decided before the second screening stays
listed: that human never saw the second verdict.  The key [[x]] was
free in this modal, and the design of the issue proposed it.

