\section{Search Filters Modal}
\label{SearchFiltersModal}

\subsection{Motivation: Why Filter in the TUI?}

The CLI already supports search filters (year, open access, venue, etc.), so why
add them to the TUI as well? Consider a typical literature review workflow:

\begin{enumerate}
\item Researcher runs [[scholar search "machine learning" --review]]
\item Reviews 50 papers, keeps 10, discards 20, leaves 20 pending
\item Realizes they only want papers from 2020 onwards
\item Without TUI filtering, must quit, re-run search with [[--year 2020-]], 
  lose progress
\end{enumerate}

By adding filter controls to the TUI, researchers can narrow down their view
without losing review progress. The TUI preserves all decisions; filtering
simply hides papers that don't match the criteria. Clearing the filters
reveals all papers again with their decisions intact.

\subsection{Design Decision: Local Filtering}

The TUI filters work entirely locally on the already-fetched results. When you
apply filters:

\begin{enumerate}
\item No new API calls are made to providers
\item Papers not matching the filter are hidden from view (not deleted)
\item All review decisions are preserved
\item Clearing filters restores the full list
\end{enumerate}

This approach has several advantages:
\begin{description}
\item[Speed] Filtering is instant since no network requests are needed.
\item[Preserves work] Review decisions are never lost when adjusting filters.
\item[Works offline] Once results are loaded, filtering works without network.
\item[Supports merged sessions] Users can merge multiple searches into one 
  session, then filter within the combined results.
\end{description}

The limitation is that filtering only works on fields present in the fetched
results. For example, if a provider didn't return citation counts, filtering
by citations won't work for those papers.

\subsection{Modal Structure}

The search filters modal follows the same pattern as other modals in the TUI,
appearing as an overlay that captures keyboard input until dismissed.

