\subsection{A human's decision: one procedure}
\label{sec:tui-human-decision}

A human decides in three ways: a row of a verdicts file, the
one-record [[sessions decide]], and a key in the TUI.  Screening a
record is one job whoever does it and however, so the three follow one
set of rules, stated once in the review module
(\cref{sec:decision-rules} there) and called from here as from the
CLI (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/349}).  The TUI
used to repeat its own version of the procedure in each of its eight
handlers: keep, keep with themes, discard and confirm, once on the
paper list and once on the detail screen.  Each set the status and the
tags, asked [[mark_as_reviewed]] whether the human agreed with a
machine's decision (by the status alone), and wrote the record of the
review.  The handlers now only collect the human's verdict, the status
and the tags chosen in the dialog, and hand it to one method of the
application, [[decide_as_human]], which both screens reach (the detail
screen through [[self.app]]).  It applies the two rules:
\begin{description}
\item[One category, and it fits its status.]  The tags the human
  chose must hold at most one category, and in a session that uses
  categories it must not contradict the verdict ([[decision_refusal]]):
  a discard does not take [[supports-claim]] or [[qualifies-claim]], a
  keep does not take [[off-topic-false-hit]].  The tag dialogs
  preselect the record's tags and return the whole set, so discarding
  a record kept under [[supports-claim]] and accepting the dialog as it
  opens would store exactly that pair, and ticking
  [[off-topic-false-hit]] as well would store both categories.  The
  decision is then not stored at all, and the TUI says why the way it
  says everything, in a notification: the words of [[sessions decide]]
  for two categories (the human unticks one; the TUI does not guess
  which was meant), or which categories fit and that a record bearing
  on the claim but excluded is discarded with the exclusion criterion
  and no category.  The detail screen stays open, so the
  human can try again.  It shows for a little longer than the default,
  since it asks the reader to act.
\item[A changed category is a change.]  On an unreviewed machine
  decision, the human confirms it only when the status and the category
  stay ([[changes_the_verdict]]), so keeping a machine's keep under
  another category is a correction, an example, as a changed status
  is.  The quick keep of a record already kept leaves its tags (below),
  so on a machine keep it is a confirmation.
\end{description}
The return value says what happened: [[None]] for a refusal, otherwise
whether a verdict was changed, which opens the reason prompt (below).

A human who decides again a decision that is already the human's, with
the same status and the same category, has not changed it: the reason
in words stays, as the tags do, since it still explains the decision
that stands.  It used to be cleared, because [[record_human_decision]]
writes a new record and a reason not given is none, and no prompt
opened to give it back.  A changed status or category is a new
decision, so the old reason goes, as before, and the prompt opens as it
does for a machine's changed decision: it is the same code path, the
[[changed]] flag, now raised for any decided record and not only a
machine's.  A pending paper decided for the first time is not a change
of anything and opens no prompt.  The words belong to whoever wrote
them, so when another named human made the decision ([[r]]'s rule,
below), deciding it again does not take them over.

