def quick_keep_tags(decision: ReviewDecision) -> list[str]:
    """The tags a quick keep leaves: a kept record's own, else none."""
    if decision.status == DecisionStatus.KEPT:
        return list(decision.tags)
    return []
@

Confirming ([[C]]) stores nothing new about the verdict: the status and
the tags stay, the source becomes the reviewed one, [[is_example]] is
cleared, and the human's record of the review is written.  What it
does store is the human's word that the machine's verdict is right.  A
machine's discard under a bearing category (an agent's one-record
decision could make one before \#349) would become a pair that a human
stands behind, which the rule refuses to a human's own decision; so
confirming such a decision is refused the same way, and the human keeps
it ([[K]]) or discards it with its criterion ([[d]]).  A decision that
holds two categories (stored before the rule, \cref{sec:decision-rules}
in the review module) is refused for the same reason, until the human
decides it again with one.  The pair stays
as stored, and displayable, until then.  Everything else is the
confirmation as it was, key for key, which the tests below pin.

<<app methods for a human's decision>>=
def confirm_as_human(self, decision: ReviewDecision,
                     reason: str | None = None) -> bool:
    """Confirm an unreviewed machine decision as the human's; say so.

    Refused, with a notification, when the decision holds a category
    that does not fit its status. `reason` is the human's, if given.
    """
    refusal = decision_refusal(self.session.decisions, decision.status,
                               decision.tags, named_by="it holds")
    if refusal is not None:
        self.notify("Confirming would make this decision yours. "
                    + refusal, severity="error",
                    timeout=RULE_NOTICE_TIMEOUT)
        return False
    from scholar.llm_review import mark_as_reviewed

    machine = machine_name(decision.source)
    mark_as_reviewed(decision, user_agrees=True)
    record_human_decision(decision, "tui", by=self.reviewer, reason=reason)
    self.notify(f"Confirmed {machine} decision: {decision.status.value}")
    return True
@

\paragraph{A reason in words.}
A decision's reason is its tags together with a reason in words when
one is given (the author's rule, \cref{sec:decision-trace} in the
review module): expected and counted, never forced.  [[sessions
decide]] takes the human's words with [[--reason]] or in a file's
column, and stores them as [[human_reason]] through
[[record_human_decision]]; the TUI had no way to give them.  The key
[[r]] (free on both screens) now opens a prompt for the current record,
[[ReasonModal]], whose words go through the same function into the same
field.  It shows what a reviewer needs to word a reason: the machine's
reason when the record has a machine's part (the trace's machine,
whether the human has reviewed it or not), the human's present reason,
and the session's earlier reasons in words to pick from.  The earlier
reasons are the humans' ([[human_reason]]), most used first, at most
[[REASON_CHOICES]] of them: a reason worth picking is one a reviewer
gives to many records (\enquote{bears on the claim, but a workshop
paper}), while a classifier's or an agent's reason is written for its
one record and would only crowd the list.  An empty entry, or Escape,
leaves the reason as it was.

What the words are attached to depends on the record:
\begin{description}
\item[A pending record] has no decision to give a reason for; the key
  says so.  Its decision with tags needs no prompt: its tags are a
  reason.
\item[A human's or a reviewed decision] gets the words and nothing
  else ([[set_human_reason]], \cref{sec:human-reason} in the review
  module): adding words to a decision is not deciding again, so who
  decided, when, through what and from which batch stay as stored.
  The words are the decider's, though, so when the TUI has a
  reviewer's name and the decision another's, the key refuses and says
  who decided; to give a reason of one's own, one decides again.  A
  record without a name, or a TUI without one, cannot tell two people
  apart and takes the words.
\item[An unreviewed machine decision] is confirmed with the words: a
  reason of the human's own, with the status and category as stored,
  is a confirmation, so the key refuses where [[C]] refuses.  The
  prompt says so in one line before the words are typed
  ([[confirms]], the status being confirmed), since a reviewer who
  only meant to note something would otherwise learn from the
  notification afterwards that the decision is now theirs; Escape
  still leaves it unconfirmed.
\end{description}
When a human changes a machine's decision (its status or its
category), the prompt opens by itself, since the change is the
decision a reader of the report will most want explained; one key,
Escape, dismisses it.  One-key confirmation ([[C]]) opens no prompt.
In blind mode, a hidden decision gets no prompt, which would show the
machine's reason.

<<tui data structures>>=
REASON_CHOICES = 5
<<tui imports>>=
from collections import Counter
<<tui blind review helpers>>=
def earlier_reasons(decisions, limit: int = REASON_CHOICES) -> list[str]:
    """The humans' reasons in words among `decisions`, most used first.

    Ties keep the order in which the reasons first occur; at most
    `limit` are returned.
    """
    counts = Counter(
        d.human_reason for d in decisions if d.is_decided and d.human_reason
    )
    return [reason for reason, _ in counts.most_common(limit)]
@

The prompt is reached from the list and from the detail screen; the
detail screen passes [[done]], its own redraw, since the paper list is
not the screen on top then and cannot be redrawn.

<<app methods for a human's decision>>=
def ask_reason(self, decision: ReviewDecision, done=None) -> None:
    """Open the reason prompt for `decision`, then store the words."""
    if not decision.is_decided:
        self.notify("Decide first (K, T or d): a reason belongs to a "
                    "decision", severity="warning")
        return
    if self.blind_refuses_confirm(decision):
        return
    if decision.source.is_unreviewed and decision_refusal(
            self.session.decisions, decision.status, decision.tags):
        self.confirm_as_human(decision)
        return
    if self.decided_by_another(decision):
        self.notify(f"Decided by {decision.human_by}: to give your own "
                    "reason, decide it again (K, T or d)",
                    severity="warning", markup=False)
        return
    machine = decision_trace(decision).machine
    self.push_screen(
        ReasonModal(
            title=decision.paper.title or "",
            machine=machine_name(decision.source),
            machine_reason=machine.reason if machine else None,
            current=decision.human_reason,
            choices=earlier_reasons(self.session.decisions),
            confirms=decision.status.value
            if decision.source.is_unreviewed else None,
        ),
        lambda words: self._take_reason(decision, words, done),
    )

def decided_by_another(self, decision: ReviewDecision) -> bool:
    """Whether a named human other than the TUI's reviewer decided it."""
    return bool(self.reviewer and decision.human_by
                and decision.human_by != self.reviewer)

def _take_reason(self, decision: ReviewDecision, words: str | None,
                 done) -> None:
    """Store the human's `words` for `decision`; None leaves it."""
    if words is None:
        return
    try:
        if decision.source.is_unreviewed:
            if not self.confirm_as_human(decision, reason=words):
                return
        else:
            set_human_reason(decision, words)
            self.notify("Reason recorded")
    except ValueError as error:
        self.notify(f"Reason not recorded: {error}", severity="error")
        return
    if done is not None:
        done()
    else:
        self._refresh_list()
        self._update_status()
@

The prompt itself is a small dialog: the record's title, the machine's
and the human's present reasons, the earlier reasons in an
[[OptionList]] and an input that has the focus.  Enter on the input
gives its text, stripped (an empty one is [[None]]); Tab moves to the
list, where Enter picks the highlighted reason; Escape gives [[None]].
Stored strings are shown as plain text, never as markup, as everywhere
else in the TUI.

<<tui reason modal>>=
class ReasonModal(ModalScreen[str | None]):
    """Ask the human's reason in words for a decision."""

    BINDINGS = [Binding("escape", "cancel", "Skip")]

    DEFAULT_CSS = """
    ReasonModal {
        align: center middle;
    }
    #reason-dialog {
        width: 80;
        height: auto;
        border: thick $primary;
        background: $surface;
        padding: 1 2;
    }
    #reason-choices {
        height: auto;
        max-height: 8;
    }
    """

    def __init__(self, title: str, machine: str, machine_reason: str | None,
                 current: str | None, choices: list[str],
                 confirms: str | None = None) -> None:
        super().__init__()
        self.confirms = confirms
        self.title_text = title
        self.machine = machine
        self.machine_reason = machine_reason
        self.current = current
        self.choices = choices

    def compose(self) -> ComposeResult:
        with Container(id="reason-dialog"):
            yield Static(Text(f"Reason: {self.title_text}", style="bold"))
            if self.confirms:
                yield Static(Text(
                    f"Giving a reason confirms the {self.machine}'s "
                    f"decision ({self.confirms}) as yours."))
            if self.machine_reason:
                yield Static(Text(
                    f"The {self.machine}'s reason: {self.machine_reason}"))
            if self.current:
                yield Static(Text(f"Your reason now: {self.current}"))
            if self.choices:
                yield Static("Earlier reasons (Tab, then Enter, to pick one):")
                yield OptionList(*(Text(c) for c in self.choices),
                                 id="reason-choices")
            yield Input(placeholder="Your reason (Enter; Escape skips)",
                        id="reason-input")

    def on_mount(self) -> None:
        self.query_one("#reason-input", Input).focus()

    def on_input_submitted(self, event: Input.Submitted) -> None:
        self.dismiss(event.value.strip() or None)

    def on_option_list_option_selected(
        self, event: OptionList.OptionSelected
    ) -> None:
        self.dismiss(self.choices[event.option_index])

    def action_cancel(self) -> None:
        self.dismiss(None)
@

\paragraph{Who decided, and why.}
The detail screen showed the tags, and the machine's reason only in a
blind review.  It now shows, for a decided record outside a blind
review, who decided ([[source_label]], the wording of [[sessions show]]
and the report; for a reviewed machine decision whether the human
confirmed or changed it) and the reason that stands, read by
[[decision_trace]]: the decider's words, or, for a confirmed machine
decision whose human gave none, the machine's.  A human's, an agent's
and a classifier's reason therefore read the same, with whose it is.
A blind review shows its own section instead (\cref{sec:tui-blind-review}),
which reads the reason the same way.

<<tui blind review helpers>>=
def decision_lines(decision: ReviewDecision) -> list[str]:
    """Who decided `decision` and the reason that stands, as plain lines."""
    if not decision.is_decided:
        return []
    trace = decision_trace(decision)
    label = source_label(decision.source)
    if trace.machine is not None and decision.source in REVIEWED_BY_A_HUMAN:
        label += ", confirmed" if trace.confirmed else ", changed"
    lines = [f"Decided by: {label}"]
    if trace.reason:
        part = trace.decider if trace.decider is not None \
            and trace.decider.reason else trace.machine
        kind = {"llm": "LLM"}.get(part.kind, part.kind)
        who = kind if part.name is None else f"{kind} {part.name}"
        lines.append(f"Reason ({who}): {trace.reason}")
    return lines
<<show who decided the paper and why>>=
lines = decision_lines(self.decision) if blind is None else []
if lines:
    yield Static("")
    yield Static("[bold]─── Decision ───[/bold]")
    for line in lines:
        yield Static(line, markup=False)
