\subsection{A notes file that cannot be read}
\label{sec:tui-unreadable-notes}

The TUI reads the notes file while it draws: the paper list marks the
papers that have a note, and the detail screen shows the note itself.
Since an unreadable [[paper_notes.json]] raises [[NotesFileError]]
instead of reading as empty (\cref{notes-module}), a broken file made
the TUI crash with a traceback as it started
(\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholar/issues/121}).  The CLI turns the
error into one line and stops (\cref{sec:cli-main}), but stopping is
the wrong answer for the TUI: the review decisions do not depend on the
notes, and a reviewer half-way through a session should be able to go
on.  So the TUI catches the error where it reads notes and goes on
without them:
\begin{description}
\item[The paper list] shows no note markers and reports the error as
  an error notification.  The list is redrawn after every decision, so
  [[read_notes_for_display]] remembers the message it last showed and
  stays quiet until the error changes or the file reads again; the
  notification itself stays longer than the default so that a message
  shown once at start-up is not missed.
\item[The detail screen] shows the message in red where the note would
  be, so it is in view whenever a note is.
\item[Editing a note] ([[n]], on either screen) is refused with the
  same message before the terminal is handed to the editor, so the user
  never types a note that could not be saved.  This is what keeps the
  notes read-only, and it costs one read of the file per key press.
  A write that fails after the edit is reported the same way, and
  the message names the file where [[keep_unsaved_note]] kept the text
  just typed (\cref{notes-module}), since the editor's own copy is
  gone by then.
\item[Migrating notes on enrichment] runs in a background thread
  (\cref{sec:abstract-screen}).  [[migrate_paper_id]] reports an
  unreadable file itself and only raises when a write fails; the thread
  turns that into the same notification instead of dying with a
  traceback over the screen.  A refused notes lock moves nothing and
  makes [[migrate_paper_id]] return [[False]]
  (\cref{sec:notes-migrate-paper-id}); its warning goes to the log,
  which the TUI does not show, so the thread shows a notification
  instead ([[notes_left_message]]) that says the note stayed under the
  old id and names the [[sessions reconcile]] command that moves it,
  as a failed note edit names the file that kept its text.  The
  adoption itself is recorded in the session as a rename when the
  enriched paper replaces the decision's ([[record_rename]],
  \cref{sec:session-renames}), on the main thread like every other
  change to the session, so the samples drawn before it still find the
  paper and that reconcile knows where the note belongs.
\end{description}
The message names the file and its [[.bak]], like the CLI's.
A write refused because another process holds the notes file's lock
([[NotesNotLocked]], \cref{sec:notes-lock}) is reported the same way,
the kept text included, but without the advice to repair the file,
which is not broken: the note can be saved again once the lock is
free.  The TUI
cannot meet the session file's [[SessionFileError]] this way: it never
reads the session file while it runs, only the caller saves it after
the TUI has closed, and [[save_session]] treats an unreadable file on
disk as nothing to merge (\cref{sec:session-integrity}).

<<tui notes helpers>>=
NOTES_ERROR_TIMEOUT = 30  # seconds; the default of 5 is easy to miss


def notes_unavailable_message(error: NotesFileError) -> str:
    """Return how the TUI reports a notes file it cannot read or write."""
    kept = (
        f"Your note was not saved; its text is in {error.unsaved_note}.\n"
        if error.unsaved_note is not None else ""
    )
    if isinstance(error, NotesNotLocked):
        return f"{error}\n{kept}Try again once the lock is free."
    return (
        f"{error}\n{kept}"
        "Notes are unavailable until the file is repaired or its previous "
        f"version is restored from {backup_path(error.path)}."
    )


def notes_left_message(session: ReviewSession, paper: Paper) -> str:
    """Return how the TUI reports a note left under a renamed paper's id."""
    from scholar.review import session_argument

    return (
        f"The note of {get_paper_id(paper)} was not moved to the adopted "
        "preprint: another scholar process holds the notes lock. Once it "
        "is free, run: scholar sessions reconcile "
        f"{shlex.quote(session_argument(session))}"
    )


def edit_note_reporting_errors(app: App, paper: Paper) -> None:
    """Edit `paper`'s note in the external editor.

    A notes file that cannot be read is reported as an error
    notification before the editor opens; a failed save is reported
    the same way. Neither raises.
    """
    try:
        get_note(paper)
        with app.suspend():
            edit_note_in_editor(paper)
    except NotesFileError as error:
        app.notify(
            notes_unavailable_message(error),
            severity="error", markup=False, timeout=NOTES_ERROR_TIMEOUT,
        )
<<app methods for unreadable notes>>=
notes_error: str | None = None

def read_notes_for_display(self) -> dict[str, PaperNote] | None:
    """Return all notes, or None if the notes file cannot be read.

    The error is shown as a notification the first time it occurs;
    later calls stay quiet until it changes or the file reads again.
    """
    try:
        notes = _get_cached_notes()
    except NotesFileError as error:
        message = notes_unavailable_message(error)
        if message != self.notes_error:
            self.notes_error = message
            self.notify(
                message,
                severity="error", markup=False, timeout=NOTES_ERROR_TIMEOUT,
            )
        return None
    self.notes_error = None
    return notes
