When the modal closes, we update the stored filters and refresh the display.
The filtering is applied during [[get_filtered_decisions]], so we just need
to store the new filters and refresh the list.

<<handle search filters result>>=
def _on_search_filters_closed(self, result: SearchFilters | None) -> None:
    """Handle search filters modal result and apply local filtering."""
    # result is None if user cancelled
    if result is None:
        self.query_one("#paper-list", ListView).focus()
        return
    
    # Update filters and refresh display
    self.search_filters = result
    self.current_index = 0
    self._refresh_list()
    self._update_status()
    
    # Notify user about filter status
    if result.is_empty():
        self.notify("Filters cleared", severity="information")
    else:
        filtered = self.get_filtered_decisions()
        self.notify(
            f"Showing {len(filtered)} of {len(self.session.decisions)} papers",
            severity="information"
        )
    self.query_one("#paper-list", ListView).focus()
@

\subsubsection{Applying Local Filters}

The actual filtering happens in [[get_filtered_decisions]], which filters by
decision status (kept/discarded/pending) and then applies bibliographic filters
using the [[filter_decisions]] function from the review module.

The [[filter_decisions]] function uses [[SearchFilters.matches()]] to check if
each paper matches the filter criteria:
\begin{description}
\item[Year] Paper's year falls within the specified range (if set)
\item[Venue] Paper's venue contains the filter string (case-insensitive, if set)
\item[Citations] Paper has at least the minimum citation count (if set)
\item[Open access] Paper is open access (if filter is enabled)
\item[Publication types] Paper's type matches one of the selected types (if any selected)
\end{description}

