\section{Blind review of a sample}
\label{sec:tui-blind-review}

The sample filter (\cref{FilterModal}) lists the papers of a review
sample that still wait for a human, each with the machine's verdict
beside it: the status mark, the classifier's or agent's mark with its
confidence, and on the detail screen its tags under \enquote{Themes} or
\enquote{Motivations}.  A reviewer who reads \enquote{discarded, 90\,\%}
before reading the abstract checks that verdict; they do not screen the
paper themselves.  Such a check is what the sample's report counts, and
it has its use, but it is not a second screen: PRISMA~2020 asks a
review to report \enquote{whether multiple reviewers worked
independently (that is, were unaware of each other's decisions)} (Page
et~al., PRISMA 2020 explanation and elaboration, BMJ 2021, n160,
item~8, doi:10.1136/bmj.n160), and a reviewer who saw the verdict was
not unaware of it.  Blind mode lets the reviewer give their own verdict
first (\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/254},
\url{https://github.com/dbosk/scholarcli/issues/253}).

\paragraph{The whole.}
A blind review starts with the TUI: [[scholar sessions resume S --blind
--by NAME]] opens the session in blind mode on the sample filter,
before any paper or verdict has been drawn on the screen.  While blind
mode is on, every paper the sample filter lists that the reviewer has
not yet screened is \emph{hidden}, and four things keep it so.
\begin{description}
\item[The paper] Its row shows a blue [[B]] in place of the status
  mark and no machine mark or confidence; its detail screen shows no
  tags and no heading (\enquote{Themes}, \enquote{Motivations}) that
  would name the status.
\item[The view] It stays on the sample filter: the decision filters of
  [[f]] (kept, discarded, pending, the machine filters, the training
  examples, the unresolved screening conflicts of [[x]]) are refused,
  since being listed under one of them is the verdict, or, for the
  conflicts, says that some screener's verdict differs from another's.  The search filters of [[F]] stay; they select by year,
  venue, citations, open access and publication type, which are the
  paper's, not the decision's ([[SearchFilters.matches]]).
\item[The counts] The status bar leaves the hidden papers out of its
  kept, discarded and pending counts and shows them as
  \enquote{Hidden}, since the totals minus the visible rows would give
  their statuses.
\item[The tag dialogs] Those of [[T]] and [[d]] start with nothing
  selected for a hidden paper, and in blind mode every tag dialog
  offers, and counts, only the tags of papers that are not hidden: the
  themes come from kept papers and the motivations from discarded ones,
  so a tag that only a hidden paper holds would name its status and
  more.
\end{description}
The TUI shows the machine's reason and the screenings nowhere outside
the reveal below.  A hidden paper is then reviewed in two steps.
\begin{enumerate}
\item The reviewer keeps or discards it as usual ([[K]], [[T]] with
  themes, [[d]] with motivations).  That verdict is stored as a
  screening of the decision (\cref{sec:screenings} in the review
  module): kind [[human]], the reviewer's name, the channel [[tui]], the
  sample that drew the paper, and [[blind]] true on the ground [[tui]]
  when blind mode has been on since the TUI started (below).  The
  decision itself is not changed.  [[record_screening]] first
  snapshots the machine's verdict, so what the machine said stays on
  record whatever the decision becomes.
\item The hidden verdict is revealed: a notification says what the
  machine decided, and the detail screen, where the reviewer stays,
  shows it in full, with the machine's reason.  The reviewer now
  confirms it ([[C]]) or changes it ([[K]], [[T]], [[d]]) through the
  existing paths, which record the human's decision as always.  That
  decision is the adjudication of the two verdicts.
\end{enumerate}
A reviewer who leaves the paper between the two steps keeps the
screening, and the decision stays unreviewed: the sample filter lists
the paper again, now revealed, until the decision is confirmed or
changed.  [[C]] on a paper still hidden is refused: the reviewer would
confirm a verdict they have not seen.

\paragraph{Blind since the start.}
[[b]] switches blind mode on and off during a run, from the paper list
only: on a detail screen or in a dialog it is refused, since that
screen was drawn with the verdict.  But a reviewer who switches blind
mode on during a run has had the list in front of them with every mark
shown, and one who switches it off and on again has seen the verdicts
in between.  Scholar cannot tell which verdicts they looked at, so it
does not claim any was hidden: a screening is recorded as blind only
when blind mode has been on without a break since the TUI started in
it ([[--blind]]).  Otherwise it is recorded with [[blind]] false and no
ground, still through the channel [[tui]], and the status bar says
\enquote{Blind (verdicts were shown earlier in this run)}.  Hiding goes
on all the same, for the verdicts the reviewer has not yet seen.

\paragraph{Which papers.}
Blind mode hides exactly the papers the sample filter lists
([[sampled_for_review]]): the unreviewed decisions drawn in any sample
of the session, a band of a banded draw and a census included, and not
reset since the draw.  A paper counts as screened by the reviewer when
its decision holds a counted screening ([[counted_screenings]]) by a
human of the reviewer's name, whether made here or with [[sessions
screen]]: once the reviewer's verdict is on record, hiding the other
one protects nothing.

\paragraph{A name first.}
A screening is one screener's verdict, told apart from another's by
kind and name, and [[sessions screen]] refuses to record one without a
name.  The TUI must not either: an unnamed human screening would have
the identity of the snapshot of any unnamed human decision, and a
comparison of screeners, which takes one verdict per identity, would
read the two as one screener's.  So blind mode cannot be on without the
reviewer's name.  It comes from [[--by]] of [[sessions resume]], the
option every command that records a person's act already uses
([[sessions decide]], [[screen]], [[relabel]], [[reset]]); without it,
blind mode asks for the name in a dialog when it starts, and leaving the
dialog empty or with Escape leaves blind mode off.  The dialog makes
[[b]] work wherever the TUI opens ([[search --review]] has neither
[[--by]] nor [[--blind]]), and it is asked once per run.  An environment
variable was rejected: it is read silently, so a name set for one
reviewer would end up on another reviewer's screenings.  The name, once
given, is also the [[human_by]] of every decision the reviewer makes in
that run (\cref{sec:abstract-llm-handling}): the confirmation that
adjudicates a blind screening is the same person's act and should carry
the same name.

A reviewer may not screen a decision they made themselves (same kind
and name as the snapshot the decision gives, [[verdict_screener]]), the
rule [[sessions screen]] enforces for the reasons given there: such a
record would be the same screener twice, and would replace the first
verdict in any comparison.  Blind mode says so and records nothing for
that paper.  Through the sample filter this cannot arise today, since a
sample queues only unreviewed machine decisions, whose screener is a
machine; the check is there so that it stays true if the samples ever
queue more.

\paragraph{What is not hidden.}
Blind mode hides what Scholar's own screens show, as listed above, and
nothing else.  It cannot know whether the reviewer read the session
file, ran [[sessions show]] or another command, or remembers the paper;
[[blind_basis]] [[tui]] says how the screening was made, not more.  Nor
can it hide what the sample itself tells: a sample drawn from discards
only ([[llm sample --discarded]]) tells the reviewer that every paper in
it was discarded, and a draw by confidence (bounds or bands) tells the
range of confidence each paper was drawn from.  The notice shown when
blind mode starts says so for the samples being screened
([[sample_giveaway]]); a sample that is to be screened blind for status
is drawn from both statuses ([[--discarded --kept]]).  The papers the
sample filter lists are themselves known to be unreviewed machine
decisions.  The help of [[sessions resume]] says what is hidden and
what is not in short.

\paragraph{Other processes.}
The TUI holds the session open for as long as the review lasts, and
another process may meanwhile reset or relabel a sampled decision.  The
TUI saves through [[save_session]] like every other command, and its
merge settles such screenings (\cref{sec:screenings}): one made while
another process reset the decision is set aside, one whose snapshot
names a source another process relabelled is corrected.  The test at
the end of this section checks the first through the save of
[[sessions resume]].

\subsection{The reviewer and their screenings}

Three questions about a decision recur below: whether the reviewer made
it, which verdict the reviewer has already given, and which sample drew
it.  They need no running application, so they are functions.

[[made_by_reviewer]] compares the screener of the decision's verdict
with a human of the reviewer's name, the comparison [[sessions screen]]
makes; a pending paper has no verdict, so nobody made it.

<<tui blind review helpers>>=
def made_by_reviewer(decision: ReviewDecision, reviewer: str | None) -> bool:
    """Whether the human `reviewer` made the verdict of `decision`."""
    screener = verdict_screener(decision)
    return decision.is_decided and \
        (screener["kind"], screener["name"]) == ("human", reviewer)
@

[[reviewer_screening]] returns the reviewer's latest counted verdict,
or [[None]].  A screening set aside by a reset does not count: after a
reset the decision is a new one, and the reviewer has not screened it.

<<tui blind review helpers>>=
def reviewer_screening(
    decision: ReviewDecision, reviewer: str | None
) -> dict | None:
    """The human `reviewer`'s latest counted screening of `decision`."""
    own = [
        r for r in counted_screenings(decision)
        if r.get("origin") == "screen" and r.get("kind") == "human"
        and r.get("name") == reviewer
    ]
    return own[-1] if own else None
@

The screening names the sample that drew the paper, so that a report
can compare the verdicts made for one sample: [[sample_holding]]
(\cref{sec:sample-giveaway} in the review module, where the screen
sheet of the CLI finds it too) gives the paper's own band of a banded
draw, and leaves out a sample whose drawn decision has been reset
since, as [[sampled_for_review]] does.

What is revealed is said in one line, used by the notification and the
detail screen: the status, the machine and its name (the model for a
classifier), the confidence and the tags.

<<tui blind review helpers>>=
def revealed_verdict(decision: ReviewDecision) -> str:
    """The verdict blind mode hid, in one line."""
    machine = machine_name(decision.source) or "human"
    name = {"LLM": decision.llm_model, "agent": decision.agent_by}.get(
        machine
    )
    text = f"{decision.status.value} by {machine}"
    if name:
        text += f" {name}"
    if decision.llm_confidence is not None and decision.source.is_machine:
        text += f" ({int(decision.llm_confidence * 100)}%)"
    if decision.tags:
        text += f": {', '.join(decision.tags)}"
    return text
@

